xgis 1

/* #2291 — per-SLICE opacity on the globe vector DRAPE.
 *
 * Purpose-built. The defect: the drape's per-show opacity lived in a shared
 * GLOBAL uniform that RasterDraper.draw rewrites per slice-layer, so on WebGPU
 * (deferred queue.writeBuffer, last-writer-wins at the single frame submit)
 * every drape-eligible slice of ONE SOURCE painted with the LAST slice's
 * opacity.
 *
 * TWO UNFILTERED LAYERS ON ONE SOURCE, and that is load-bearing, not incidental:
 * map.ts:3657 gives a FILTERED layer its own VT source — hence its own
 * VectorDrapeRenderer and its own global uniform — so a filter-split fixture has
 * one slice per source and CANNOT reach the defect. Measured: with the fix cut,
 * a two-quad filter-split scene still passed. Unfiltered layers reuse the source,
 * which is the multi-slice shape the bug lives in.
 *
 * Sharing a source means the two layers cover the SAME pixels, so they are
 * separated by COLOUR rather than by position: red under, green over, both
 * translucent over an opaque black background. The composite then encodes BOTH
 * opacities independently —
 *
 *     correct (0.9 red, then 0.2 green)   ->  r = 0.8*0.9*255 = 184, g = 51
 *     both collapsed to the last (0.2)    ->  r =  0.8*0.2*255 =  41, g = 51
 *     both collapsed to the first (0.9)   ->  r =  0.1*0.9*255 =  23, g = 230
 *
 * — three states that are far apart in (r, g), where a single-colour fixture
 * would put the correct value and one of the collapsed values within a few
 * levels of each other.
 *
 * No stroke: an outline draws through the LINE pipeline, which carries its own
 * opacity, and mixing both consumers would make a red ambiguous about which one
 * collapsed.
 *
 * Geometry is pushed via setSourceData after run() — this container has no
 * network egress.
 */

background { fill: #000000 }

source poly { type: geojson }

layer fill_under {
  source: poly
  | fill-#ff0000 stroke-0 opacity-90
}

layer fill_over {
  source: poly
  | fill-#00ff00 stroke-0 opacity-20
}
